.TH BAO\-KIT 1 "2026-09-06" "NuCode" "LAN vault"
.SH NAME
bao-kit \- the personal-Vault workflow wrapper
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bao-kit
is a single bash script wrapping the whole personal-Vault workflow for an OpenBao instance on the LAN.
It supports one operator (ziggy), per\-project secrets, per\-session tokens, and tightly scoped subagent tokens.
It is personal\-grade by design.
It always exports
.B BAO_ADDR
and
.B BAO_CACERT
and sets
.B BAO_FORMAT=json
before running
.BR bao .
If
.B BAO_TOKEN
is unset and
.I ~/.config/openbao/token
is readable, it loads that cached token and silently renews it.
Secrets never print unless you explicitly ask (the
.BR unwrap
command, or running
.BR "bao kv get"
yourself).
Usage:
.BR "bao-kit COMMAND [args]" .
It requires
.BR bao
and
.BR python3
on
.BR PATH .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Setup and authentication
.TP
.BI env
Print shell exports:
.B BAO_ADDR ,
.B BAO_CACERT ,
and
.B BAO_TOKEN
if a login is cached.
Use as:
.nf
eval "$(bao\-kit env)"
.fi
.TP
.BI init
One\-time setup.
Initialises the vault with a single unseal key share, unseals it, enables the kv v2 secrets engine at
.BR kv/ ,
enables userpass auth, enables the file audit device, and writes the operator policy.
Writes the unseal key and root token to
.B ~/.config/openbao/init.json
at mode 600.
.TP
.BI unseal
Morning ritual: the PC sleeps at night and the vault seals, so unseal it with the stored key from
.BR init.json ,
then prints status.
.TP
.BI user
Create or reset the ziggy operator user.
Prompts for a password, so run it in a terminal.
The user gets the operator policy and 12h tokens (24h max).
.TP
.BI login
Log in as ziggy.
Prompts for the password, obtains a 12h operator token, and caches it in
.BR ~/.config/openbao/token .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Projects and secrets
.TP
.BI project " NAME"
Create or refresh the policy project\-NAME, granting read on kv/NAME/*, and a token role project\-NAME (orphan service tokens, 24h max) that mints session tokens carrying that policy.
.TP
.BI put " NAME/KEY field=value ..."
Store a secret at kv/NAME/KEY. It merges the given fields into an existing secret (it patches; it does not replace the whole secret), and creates the secret if absent.
.TP
.BI status
Print seal state and health (sealed, initialized, version) and, if a token is loaded, who am I (display name, policies, ttl, metadata).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Sessions and delegation
.TP
.BI session NAME \-\- CMD...
Mint an 8h token scoped to project NAME (policy project-NAME), run CMD with BAO_TOKEN, BAO_SESSION and BAO_PROJECT set in its environment, and revoke the token when CMD exits. CMD defaults to claude. Revocation cascades to any child tokens the session minted.
.TP
.BI sub [TTL] [USES]
Run from inside a session. Mint a use-limited child token for a subagent. TTL defaults to 30m, USES defaults to 2.
.TP
.BI wrap NAME/KEY [TTL]
Print a single-use response-wrapping token that carries the secret at kv/NAME/KEY. TTL defaults to 5m. Hand the token to a subagent.
.TP
.BI unwrap TOKEN
The subagent side of wrap. Unwrap the token and print the secret fields as KEY=VALUE lines. This is one of the two commands that print a secret.
.TP
.BI with NAME/KEY:field=ENV[,field=ENV] \-\- CMD...
Read the secret at kv/NAME/KEY and run CMD with each named field placed in the child's environment as the given ENV variable. The secret is only ever in the child's environment, never printed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BAO_ADDR
The vault address. Defaults to
.BR https://192.168.88.18:8200 .
.TP
.B BAO_CACERT
Path to the self\-signed CA cert. Defaults to the
.B tls/bao.crt
file under the bao\-kit install directory.
.TP
.B BAO_FORMAT
bao\-kit sets this to
.B json
internally so it can parse output. Note that with
.B json
format, bao's
.BR \-field=
prints a quoted string.
.TP
.B BAO_TOKEN
The auth token. If set in the environment it takes precedence over the cached token file (this is deliberate for scoped sessions, but a STALE exported
.B BAO_TOKEN
will shadow a fresh login; see NOTES).
.TP
.B BAO_SESSION , BAO_PROJECT
Set by the session command in the child environment, naming the session id and project.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI ~/.config/openbao/init.json
The unseal key and root token from init, mode 600. Required by unseal.
.TP
.BI ~/.config/openbao/token
The cached operator login token, mode 600, loaded when BAO_TOKEN is unset.
.TP
.BI tls/bao.crt
The self-signed CA certificate, under the install directory.
.TP
.BI policies/operator.hcl
The operator policy, written by init.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B Stale BAO_TOKEN
A stale BAO_TOKEN in the shell shadows a fresh login. bao\-kit only loads the cached token file when BAO_TOKEN is unset, so an exported token left over from a previous session keeps being used and every call fails with a 403 even right after login. Fix: unset BAO_TOKEN before the command.
.TP
.B put merges fields
put merges fields into an existing secret rather than replacing it, so adding one field does not erase the others. (A plain bao kv put would replace the whole secret.)
.TP
.B Non\-root token restrictions
A non\-root token can only mint child tokens whose policies are a subset of its own; sessions mint through the token role project\-NAME created by the project command.
.TP
.B Vault sealing
The vault seals whenever the host reboots; unseal is a daily step.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
The raw OpenBao CLI is
.BR bao (1) .
The local deployment is documented in
.BR bao (1)
as configured here (same box).
The vault repo README at
.B /data/NuCode/Bao/README.md
is authoritative.
Read this page without installing it:
.B man ./docs/bao-kit.1
